# LF everywhere, stated rather than assumed.
#
# Every repository on this platform already stored LF; what was missing was
# anything that said so. Nothing else catches a flip -- `ruff format --check`,
# `ruff check`, mypy and the test suites all pass on either ending -- so a tool
# that renormalises a file turns a one-line edit into a whole-file diff and no
# check complains. Three files in notify had drifted to CRLF before anyone
# counted bytes, and one of the three was written that way deliberately, by
# someone sampling the convention with `grep -c $'\r'` and believing the
# numbers it returned. Do not check endings that way; count bytes in the
# committed blob.
#
# `text=auto` leaves git's binary detection alone, so a tracked binary is
# still never rewritten, and `eol=lf` is what lands in the working tree --
# which is the half that matters on Windows, where the drift came from.
* text=auto eol=lf

# A CRLF shell script is not a slow-burning style problem. The container fails
# immediately and opaquely: `#!/bin/sh^M: no such file or directory`, or an
# entrypoint whose `set -e` is really `set -e\r`. Stated separately from the
# blanket rule above so that narrowing that rule cannot quietly take this with
# it.
*.sh text eol=lf
